{'id': '2608.07230', 'categories': ['cs.AI'], 'pdf': 'https://arxiv.org/pdf/2608.07230', 'abs': 'https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.07230', 'authors': ['Zora Wurm', 'Kilian Rückschloß', 'Felix Weitkämper'], 'title': 'From probability to causality in probabilistic logic programming', 'comment': 'Accepted and presented at IJCLR 2025', 'summary': 'Probabilistic logic programming is a formalism of statistical relational artificial intelligence that supports causal queries, including interventions from outside the system. When the structure of a probabilistic logic program is learned from data, however, only probabilistic information is used, and a single probability distribution may be compatible with several causal orders. This leads to ambiguity in interventional reasoning, raising the question of when the causal order is uniquely determined by the distribution. Exploiting the relationship between acyclic probabilistic logic programs and Bayesian networks, we derive conditions under which the probabilistic information encoded in a program determines a unique causal order. We also incorporate constraints arising from relational structure by taking into account prescribed sets of causal symmetries induced by the underlying relational vocabulary. The result is a method for verifying when a learned probabilistic logic program supports well-defined intervention semantics.'}
