{'id': '2608.07528', 'categories': ['cs.AI', 'cs.CL', 'cs.LG'], 'pdf': 'https://arxiv.org/pdf/2608.07528', 'abs': 'https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.07528', 'authors': ['Jyotin Goel', 'Ipshita Bandyopadhyay', 'Justin Shenk'], 'title': 'The Knowing-Saying Gap: When Probes See Errors that Confidence Misses', 'comment': None, 'summary': 'Linear probes detect corrupted context in language models with near-perfect accuracy, yet this does not translate into reliable failure prediction. The result is a dissociation with direct implications for deployment monitoring. Across multi-hop arithmetic chains, probes that detect corruption turn out to be uninformative about final answer correctness; models forced into structured confidence formats collapse to two values with indistinguishable error rates; and probe persistence across hops fails to separate correct from incorrect outcomes, refuting our pre-registered "persistence beats peak" hypothesis. This pattern of knowing but not saying generalises across model families including reasoning models. As a real-time monitor, probe-based interventions are sharply model and error-type dependent: branch-and-pick is net-positive across models and uniquely non-breaking on Llama-3.1-8B (4 rescued, 0 broken), while reprompt and replace-prior break correct traces at roughly the rate they rescue wrong ones. Probe-based monitoring is a necessary complement to verbalised confidence, but no single intervention dominates, and the deployable answer is model-aware, error-type-aware routing.'}
