{'id': '2608.08026', 'categories': ['cs.AI'], 'pdf': 'https://arxiv.org/pdf/2608.08026', 'abs': 'https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.08026', 'authors': ['Eunna Lee'], 'title': 'The Authority Expectancy Effect in Multi-User Conflict', 'comment': None, 'summary': 'We investigate how social authority (SA) signals interact with severity-based prioritization in large language models, operationalizing each axis as a model-elicited baseline -- the triage hierarchy and the SA hierarchy. Across four LLMs (Claude, Gemini, GPT, Grok) and three experimental phases -- resource allocation, fault attribution, and multi-turn dispute mediation -- we find that occupational authority, institutional documentation, and relational congruence can restructure model judgments in ways not captured by additive reweighting of authority cues. We formalize this pattern as the Authority Expectancy Effect (AEE) and characterize it through three properties observed across our conditions: it is reference-dependent, defined only relative to a pre-authority baseline; it involves evidential reinterpretation, in which identical content acquires different inferential implications depending on which party bears the SA signal; and it exhibits direction sensitivity, producing opposite outcomes depending on whether authority position and evidentiary cues align.'}
