{'id': '2608.08580', 'categories': ['cs.CV'], 'pdf': 'https://arxiv.org/pdf/2608.08580', 'abs': 'https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.08580', 'authors': ['Junsu Kim', 'Seungryul Baek'], 'title': 'Where Is the Bee? Detecting Tiny Pollinators with a Single Collaborative-Head Transformer', 'comment': "The 1st rank method's paper at CVPPA@ECCV 2026 BuzzSpot Challenge", 'summary': 'The CVPPA@ECCV 2026 BuzzSpot Challenge asks us to detect bees, bumblebees, hoverflies, and moths in 1920x1080 field keyframes. Its annotations carry 2 difficulties: the median box occupies 0.16% of a frame, and bees account for 80% of the labels. To cope with the small boxes, we compare 10 recorded detector configurations on held-out keyframes; plain Co-DINO with a Swin-L backbone has the highest mAP in this comparison, so we select it. Training then addresses the bee dominance in 2 ways: fine-tuning on a crop-mosaic pool in which the combined annotation share of the 3 rare classes rises from 19.9% to 55.1%, and a class-weighted simplex equiangular tight frame (ETF) loss that pulls the projected states of matched decoder queries toward fixed class directions. The full schedule spans 12+3+2 epochs. Without inference-time ensembling or test-time augmentation, we rank first on FinalTest at 0.5062 mAP@[.5:.95].'}
